Styling: Match existing dashboard (dark navy bg, white cards, Tailwind CSS)

SALES LOG TABLE VIEW:
- Accessed via "View Sales Log" button (add to ManagerDashboard)
- Shows all logged deals in table format
- Columns: Date, Deal#, Advisor, Vehicle, Front Gross, Backend Total, Commission, Split, Actions
- Filterable by: Date range, Advisor, Vehicle type
- Sortable by: Date, Commission
- Actions: [Edit] [Delete] buttons per row
- Pagination: 25 rows per page
- Export to CSV button

Edit functionality:
- Opens LogSaleForm in edit mode
- Pre-fills all fields from existing sale
- Tracks edit history (who, when, what changed)
- Requires reason for edit (dropdown + notes)

Delete functionality:
- Opens DeleteSaleModal
- Requires reason (dropdown: Return, Unwind, Duplicate, Error, Other)
- Requires notes (min 10 chars)
- Soft delete (marks deleted=true)
- Updates advisor commission totals
- Shows confirmation with impact warning

MONTHLY PLAYBOOK - GOAL SETTING:

GoalSettingForm.jsx is a multi-step wizard (6 steps).

STEP 1: Team Setup & Overview
- List active advisors with stats
- [+ Add Advisor] button
- Monthly targets (units, gross, backend PVR)

STEP 2: Inventory & Forecast
- New cars on ground, coming this month
- Model-by-model forecast (QX50/55/60/80 with targets)

STEP 3: Backend Breakdown
- Financing reserve target
- Product-by-product targets (Maintenance, Gap, etc.)

STEP 4: Pre-owned Targets
- CPO vs Non-CPO forecast

STEP 5: Aging Inventory
- 10 oldest cars to move (stock numbers)

STEP 6: Review & Submit
- Show complete playbook summary
- Require GM/GSM password to submit

PASSWORD PROTECTION:
- After Step 6 "Submit Goals" clicked, show password modal
- Hardcoded passwords for now:
  - GM password: "gm2026"
  - GSM password: "gsm2026"
- If wrong password: Show error, stay on Step 6
- If correct: Submit to Firebase, lock playbook

LOCKED PLAYBOOK:
- Once submitted, goals cannot be edited
- To modify: Require password again + edit reason
- Show "Last modified by [Role] on [Date]" at top
- Edit history tracked in database

DATA STRUCTURE:
goals {
  dealership_id,
  month,
  submitted_at,
  submitted_by_role, // "GM" or "GSM"
  locked: true,
  edit_history: [...],
  advisors: [...],
  inventory: {...},
  forecast: {...},
  backend_targets: {...},
  preowned_targets: {...},
  aging_inventory: [...]
}

Code style:

- Functional components, hooks only
- Clear variable names (no abbreviations)
- Extract reusable logic early
- Prioritize readability over cleverness

Project context:

- Building an automotive-themed dashboard (Dealer Dash) for multi-dealership management, where managers view metrics and goals for their own dealership and owners access cross-dealership performance comparisons.
- Tracking: unit sales, gross profit, backend PVR, goals
- Manager goal-setting by 5th of month
- Color-coded alerts, automotive-themed UI (speedometers, thermometers)
- Cross-location comparisons

CRITICAL RULES - ALWAYS FOLLOW:

- Never modify Firebase/Supabase config files without explicit permission
- Never delete API keys, auth tokens, or environment variables
- Never refactor working real-time sync logic unless asked
- Always preserve existing data structure when modifying components
- Flag breaking changes before implementing them

PROTECTED COMPONENTS:

1. GoalSettingForm.jsx - Goal calculation logic (unit sales, gross, backend PVR)
2. ManagerDashboard.jsx - Manager auth/permissions, data filtering by dealership
3. OwnerDashboard.jsx - Cross-dealership aggregation queries
4. LoginScreen.jsx - Auth flow and session management
5. Speedometer.jsx, Thermometer.jsx, TORQGauge.jsx - Visualization thresholds and color logic

MODIFICATION RULES FOR PROTECTED COMPONENTS:

- Show full diff before changing calculation logic
- Preserve existing useEffect cleanup functions (real-time listeners)
- Never change prop interfaces without checking all parent components
- Keep color thresholds intact unless explicitly asked to modify

WHEN REFACTORING:

- Extract shared logic to /utils or custom hooks
- Don't touch working dashboard queries without permission
- Maintain backwards compatibility with existing Firebase/Supabase data schema

FILE STRUCTURE EXPECTATIONS:

- /components = Reusable UI (gauges, forms, auth)
- /assets = Static resources (react.svg)
- App.css = Global styles, keep Tailwind config separate
- Keep component files under 300 lines (suggest splits if longer)

When helping:

- Give complete, working code blocks
- Flag common Firebase/Supabase pitfalls (listener cleanup, query limits)
- Suggest performance optimizations for real-time data
- Keep components small and focused
- Always test color-coded alert logic against threshold ranges
